WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.280
We've danced around this topic a lot at this point,

00:02.280 --> 00:04.440
and I love to address this.

00:04.440 --> 00:07.360
So as a recap on the context of what we're talking about

00:07.360 --> 00:10.160
is, at this point, we're talking about children components

00:10.160 --> 00:12.160
trying to figure out how the heck to update the parent

00:12.160 --> 00:15.320
component, because we've talked about the fact that, well,

00:15.320 --> 00:16.520
we don't want to mutate props.

00:16.520 --> 00:18.920
As we can see very clearly here, we're being yelled at.

00:18.920 --> 00:21.360
Don't mutate props.

00:21.360 --> 00:24.560
On the other hand, we talked about passing functions down,

00:24.560 --> 00:28.800
as in, like, if there was a method on this component that

00:28.800 --> 00:34.600
was called changeName, and call it this.userData.name equals,

00:34.600 --> 00:37.480
let's say, there's an argument for newName,

00:37.480 --> 00:39.200
and then just like that.

00:39.200 --> 00:42.120
And so then you'd be like, OK, well, inside of user,

00:42.120 --> 00:45.760
I'm going to pass this method, call it changeName,

00:45.760 --> 00:48.920
and then it'll take on the reference to that method.

00:48.920 --> 00:54.080
And then we have this changeName, which is a function.

00:54.080 --> 00:57.280
And then it's, let's say, requiredTrue.

00:57.280 --> 01:00.840
And then let's say we have a button here that's like, hey,

01:00.840 --> 01:02.880
like, changeName.

01:02.880 --> 01:05.320
That's like most people's instinctual way

01:05.320 --> 01:08.040
in how a lot of frameworks approach this problem,

01:08.040 --> 01:10.200
because the parent is telling it what to change,

01:10.200 --> 01:13.400
and the child obediently does it.

01:13.400 --> 01:15.240
The thing about this mental model, though,

01:15.240 --> 01:17.880
is that it foregoes the fact that, actually, if we think

01:17.880 --> 01:20.440
about how the web is designed, when

01:20.440 --> 01:23.640
we have childdom elements, what do we have it do?

01:23.640 --> 01:25.960
We have it bubble up events.

01:25.960 --> 01:28.440
That's actually how the web is built natively,

01:28.440 --> 01:29.480
if we think about it.

01:29.480 --> 01:31.680
It's not actually the parent just telling it what to do.

01:31.680 --> 01:34.240
It's saying, when you know that a button at a child

01:34.240 --> 01:36.360
has happened, it bubbles up that event.

01:36.360 --> 01:38.360
That's where we get these concepts, right?

01:38.360 --> 01:39.560
That, oh, a click has happened.

01:39.560 --> 01:42.680
Therefore, how does this relate to the chain of communication?

01:42.680 --> 01:44.600
And so what we're going to talk about right now,

01:44.600 --> 01:46.640
basically at this point, is how we can basically

01:46.640 --> 01:48.840
emit custom events from our child

01:48.840 --> 01:52.120
to tell the parent that something is happening.

01:52.120 --> 01:54.560
So let's actually go ahead and just take this user card

01:54.560 --> 01:55.080
example.

01:55.080 --> 01:55.800
Why not?

01:55.800 --> 01:57.040
Why wouldn't we?

01:57.040 --> 02:00.040
So let's go ahead and just set this context up a bit.

02:00.040 --> 02:04.520
So let me just remove this and how we would do this.

02:04.520 --> 02:07.880
Actually, I think I might be able to leave that.

02:07.880 --> 02:12.560
We can, for now, let's do this.

02:12.560 --> 02:14.800
Change name.

02:14.800 --> 02:17.480
So let's make sure everything's at least rendering correctly

02:17.480 --> 02:20.600
on our side for the app.

02:20.600 --> 02:21.720
OK.

02:21.720 --> 02:24.400
So let's start with something simple,

02:24.400 --> 02:26.680
which is that if we click a button from the child,

02:26.680 --> 02:29.000
it will basically tell the parent that, hey,

02:29.000 --> 02:30.840
I want to change the name.

02:30.840 --> 02:32.960
And that when that happens, what we want it to do

02:32.960 --> 02:36.200
is this is updated directly to Charlie.

02:36.200 --> 02:39.200
So we won't even worry about arguments right now.

02:39.200 --> 02:41.680
And so again, right here, just to show that this works,

02:41.680 --> 02:47.360
if we add the click and we add change name, change now,

02:47.360 --> 02:50.640
you'll see Ben does switch to Charlie as expected.

02:50.640 --> 02:52.800
OK, as expected.

02:52.800 --> 02:57.080
But this method is existing all the way up here.

02:57.080 --> 03:00.000
So how the heck does a user card get access to that

03:00.000 --> 03:03.120
without passing the prop down as a function?

03:03.120 --> 03:05.840
Well, the way we do that is with this concept,

03:05.840 --> 03:11.480
new to view 3 did not exist in View2 called emits.

03:11.480 --> 03:13.120
And emits is the equivalent of props.

03:13.120 --> 03:15.960
Props is saying, hey, this is what I'm expecting to send down.

03:15.960 --> 03:17.640
Emits is our way of documenting, hey,

03:17.640 --> 03:20.440
this is what I'm going to send up.

03:20.440 --> 03:22.120
So if you think about it, this is actually

03:22.120 --> 03:25.720
following the one-way data chain of unidirectional data

03:25.720 --> 03:28.840
flow because the data comes down and then you're basically

03:28.840 --> 03:32.040
sending a signal up to say, hey, I want something to change.

03:32.040 --> 03:35.360
Then basically the parent knows, oh, change it,

03:35.360 --> 03:37.840
then send that change back down.

03:37.840 --> 03:40.800
So in that regard, it follows that traditional web model.

03:40.800 --> 03:43.280
And so to show you what that means, we'll say emits.

03:43.280 --> 03:47.480
So again, emit the event change name.

03:47.480 --> 03:48.840
And so the key thing with the emits

03:48.840 --> 03:50.600
is to think of this as custom events.

03:50.600 --> 03:52.400
We're used to click, key up.

03:52.400 --> 03:55.080
Those are events that are already defined for us.

03:55.080 --> 03:58.480
This is a custom event that we're emitting.

03:58.480 --> 04:03.240
And so what we get to do then is once we have our emits defined,

04:03.240 --> 04:06.800
what we get to do is we get to call a global method called

04:06.800 --> 04:08.600
emit, dollar sign emit.

04:08.600 --> 04:10.360
And so if this is your first time using View,

04:10.360 --> 04:12.800
you're probably wondering, what is this dollar sign here?

04:12.800 --> 04:17.120
It's basically View's way of name scoping a global function

04:17.120 --> 04:19.080
that is available to View specifically.

04:19.080 --> 04:21.440
So now that this is not just something in JavaScript,

04:21.440 --> 04:23.600
this is a View thing, we're going to emit what?

04:23.600 --> 04:25.200
We're going to emit change name.

04:28.200 --> 04:36.520
And this is now where the DevTools become very handy.

04:36.520 --> 04:38.400
Inside the DevTools, there is this tab right here

04:38.400 --> 04:39.800
called a Timeline.

04:39.800 --> 04:41.800
And what it allows you to do is actually notice

04:41.800 --> 04:43.160
when different events are happening.

04:43.160 --> 04:45.200
So you notice that if I just click on the page here,

04:45.200 --> 04:47.960
it actually does give me a timeline of snapshot

04:47.960 --> 04:49.080
of what's happening.

04:49.080 --> 04:51.880
It even actually has the coordinates on here I am noticing.

04:51.880 --> 04:54.240
And so you see, it actually tells you the actual position

04:54.240 --> 04:55.520
of what's happening.

04:55.520 --> 04:56.680
And so when it comes to debugging,

04:56.680 --> 04:59.040
this is pretty darn useful.

04:59.040 --> 05:01.600
And so you notice here, though, if I click Change Name,

05:01.600 --> 05:02.880
something happens here.

05:02.880 --> 05:04.120
So let me scroll over.

05:04.120 --> 05:05.960
Oop.

05:05.960 --> 05:07.280
Oof.

05:07.280 --> 05:08.560
I think it's losing the scroll.

05:08.560 --> 05:10.320
OK, it's right over here.

05:10.320 --> 05:11.760
Oop.

05:11.760 --> 05:13.720
I think I zoomed out too much.

05:13.720 --> 05:15.760
OK.

05:15.760 --> 05:16.840
Refresh.

05:16.840 --> 05:18.640
OK, there we go.

05:18.640 --> 05:29.520
Where is my sidebar view reload components timeline?

05:29.520 --> 05:30.120
Oh, I hit it.

05:30.120 --> 05:30.600
Here we go.

05:30.600 --> 05:31.080
Oh my gosh.

05:31.080 --> 05:32.720
And I was like, where did it go?

05:32.720 --> 05:35.240
OK, click Change Name.

05:35.240 --> 05:36.560
And we'll see a couple of things here.

05:36.560 --> 05:39.080
One, you see right here?

05:39.080 --> 05:41.640
Change Name event has been omitted.

05:41.640 --> 05:44.520
And so it's been omitted by user card.

05:44.520 --> 05:47.400
And so you also know that it happened with a click as well.

05:47.400 --> 05:49.480
Because you see that here, it has the mouse up,

05:49.480 --> 05:51.240
mouse down, the click event.

05:51.240 --> 05:53.880
And then here is where we get that Change Name event.

05:53.880 --> 05:55.840
And you notice here, it actually tells you a little bit more

05:55.840 --> 05:56.720
about the event info.

05:56.720 --> 05:58.480
That is from the component user card.

05:58.480 --> 06:00.080
The event is Change Name.

06:00.080 --> 06:01.760
But also, we haven't gotten into this yet.

06:01.760 --> 06:03.280
We can actually pass parameters up.

06:03.280 --> 06:05.040
Because sometimes you're going to be customizing stuff

06:05.040 --> 06:06.240
at the child level.

06:06.240 --> 06:08.400
But you need to say, hey, here are some of the customizations

06:08.400 --> 06:09.560
I'm sending up to the parent.

06:09.560 --> 06:11.760
And this is what I'm going to send.

06:11.760 --> 06:13.440
And so when you do this, what happens

06:13.440 --> 06:20.240
is on your component, we get the ability to listen to what?

06:20.240 --> 06:21.760
Listen to our custom event, which is what?

06:21.760 --> 06:23.200
Change Name.

06:23.200 --> 06:26.120
So this is no different than our at click, at key up.

06:26.120 --> 06:28.440
So I'm actually going to go ahead and just do the at.

06:28.440 --> 06:30.400
Because that's my preferred.

06:30.400 --> 06:33.360
And then from here, what are we going to do when this is called?

06:33.360 --> 06:35.160
We're going to run the method changeName.

06:38.160 --> 06:39.080
OK, there we go.

06:39.080 --> 06:40.040
I'm going to run dev.

06:44.000 --> 06:45.040
There we go.

06:45.040 --> 06:47.760
Yeah, I figured.

06:47.760 --> 06:49.560
So this takes some getting used to.

06:49.560 --> 06:51.640
Because I know that, again, this is not traditionally

06:51.640 --> 06:52.960
how people are thinking of things.

06:52.960 --> 06:55.480
But it's funny when we think about the primitives

06:55.480 --> 06:57.560
were given when building with the web.

06:57.560 --> 07:00.360
Event bubbling is a very standard way of building with things.

07:00.360 --> 07:04.040
And more importantly, I think it allows for,

07:04.040 --> 07:07.360
I'll call it a cleaner way of removing

07:07.360 --> 07:11.480
that strict instructions that you're passing down

07:11.480 --> 07:13.560
from the parent when you just pass the function.

07:13.560 --> 07:15.880
Because now, all of a sudden, that function,

07:15.880 --> 07:17.680
I've just seen on code bases when people do that,

07:17.680 --> 07:20.440
it just gets hairy in terms of people,

07:20.440 --> 07:22.080
then they start passing in the wrong function.

07:22.080 --> 07:23.120
Because then you have to make sure

07:23.120 --> 07:25.800
the function is doing what you want it to in the child, which

07:25.800 --> 07:27.800
means you actually now have to check the parent

07:27.800 --> 07:29.800
to make sure that it makes sense.

07:29.800 --> 07:32.240
Whereas when you just emit an event,

07:32.280 --> 07:35.480
this child component can be used in a lot of different contexts.

07:35.480 --> 07:37.680
And then the parent can do whatever it wants with it.

07:37.680 --> 07:39.200
And it makes sense because you're saying,

07:39.200 --> 07:40.760
when the child component has this custom event,

07:40.760 --> 07:42.960
I'm going to run this function, which lives in this file.

07:42.960 --> 07:44.160
And so in that regard, it really does

07:44.160 --> 07:47.160
help to really encapsulate that single file component

07:47.160 --> 07:50.680
mentality while still building on web standards

07:50.680 --> 07:53.360
and principles that we're actually

07:53.360 --> 07:55.840
using on a day-to-day, but often overlook.

07:55.840 --> 07:56.320
Yeah.

07:56.320 --> 07:58.920
If you were to bubble up multiple levels,

07:58.920 --> 08:02.000
would you have to catch it, re-emit it?

08:02.480 --> 08:04.880
You can't just say emit, emit, emit, emit the whole way up?

08:04.880 --> 08:05.960
You cannot.

08:05.960 --> 08:07.760
And so that's a great question.

08:07.760 --> 08:09.480
Because OK, so let me reiterate.

08:09.480 --> 08:11.280
So the question here is that right now we just

08:11.280 --> 08:13.840
have a single parent, single child, one emit, whatever.

08:13.840 --> 08:16.040
What happens when you have a great, great, great, great,

08:16.040 --> 08:17.880
great, great, great, great grandchild wanting

08:17.880 --> 08:19.960
to communicate info up?

08:19.960 --> 08:23.520
And so emits and props really are designed

08:23.520 --> 08:26.440
for a closer relationship, like when you're closer together.

08:26.440 --> 08:28.520
Because props actually have that same problem.

08:28.520 --> 08:29.840
And so the pattern that's actually

08:29.840 --> 08:31.280
the anti-pattern that's resulted from that

08:31.280 --> 08:32.520
is called prop drilling.

08:32.520 --> 08:34.440
And so we have app.view has this global thing,

08:34.440 --> 08:36.080
and then you drill, drill, drill, drill,

08:36.080 --> 08:38.120
prop, prop, prop, prop, and none of the components

08:38.120 --> 08:39.760
use it until this one.

08:39.760 --> 08:41.440
But then the problem with prop drilling

08:41.440 --> 08:43.720
is that the moment you remove one in the middle,

08:43.720 --> 08:45.520
the whole thing just falls apart immediately.

08:45.520 --> 08:47.680
The answer to this is basically using

08:47.680 --> 08:50.640
composables and state management in a way that,

08:50.640 --> 08:52.360
because at that point, I would argue,

08:52.360 --> 08:54.560
if you have data that is shared across components

08:54.560 --> 08:56.920
at that depth, it probably makes sense

08:56.920 --> 08:58.760
for it to be encapsulated in a module that can just

08:58.760 --> 09:00.600
be shared across both files.

09:00.600 --> 09:02.560
The props is really, in my mind, designed

09:02.560 --> 09:05.760
when there's a tight coupling between the two components,

09:05.760 --> 09:07.840
and you want it to behave a certain way.

09:07.840 --> 09:10.200
Otherwise, what we'll see here later on

09:10.200 --> 09:12.320
is around other flexible APIs that

09:12.320 --> 09:16.160
allow it to do more than just these rigid instructions

09:16.160 --> 09:17.320
between components.

09:17.320 --> 09:20.280
Does that help to answer your question?

09:20.280 --> 09:23.880
Is there an emit equivalent in view 2?

09:23.880 --> 09:26.440
OK, so good question.

09:26.440 --> 09:29.200
In regards to view 2, the dollar sign emit actually

09:29.200 --> 09:30.560
does exist as a function.

09:31.480 --> 09:33.840
Custom events are not new to view 3.

09:33.840 --> 09:35.320
What is new to view 3 is the fact

09:35.320 --> 09:39.400
that you can basically document them inside of your options.

09:39.400 --> 09:42.520
Because the problem, and this was a misstep on,

09:42.520 --> 09:44.480
I guess on our part, we didn't think about it,

09:44.480 --> 09:46.400
is that when you don't have this emits property,

09:46.400 --> 09:48.080
it seems like a small thing.

09:48.080 --> 09:50.440
But if you start emitting multiple events,

09:50.440 --> 09:53.240
it starts getting really hairy to track in a big component

09:53.240 --> 09:55.240
of like, wait, what events are happening?

09:55.240 --> 09:55.760
Where is it?

09:55.760 --> 09:58.360
I mean, that's why we have prop definitions, too, actually.

09:58.400 --> 10:00.800
It's to let you know, hey, what am I expecting to receive?

10:00.800 --> 10:02.840
The emit does the opposite for us,

10:02.840 --> 10:04.880
where what kind of events are you expecting to emit

10:04.880 --> 10:07.360
so that people don't just randomly create their own?

10:07.360 --> 10:10.840
And so to be clear, and just to kind of wrap that up,

10:10.840 --> 10:13.160
if you don't like emitting directly here,

10:13.160 --> 10:18.960
we can actually just do something like a tell parent

10:18.960 --> 10:20.720
change name.

10:20.720 --> 10:24.600
And this can just be a method, tell parent change name.

10:24.600 --> 10:26.680
And we can do this dot dollar sign emit.

10:26.680 --> 10:28.440
This would be the same exact thing.

10:28.440 --> 10:30.600
It's up to you whether you want to call it here,

10:30.600 --> 10:32.960
or you just want to call it inline.

10:32.960 --> 10:35.360
Once again, I think that's more of a stylistic thing.

10:35.360 --> 10:36.880
Because some people would be like, well,

10:36.880 --> 10:39.040
if it's just this one event, and I'm not tracking that much,

10:39.040 --> 10:40.280
I'm just going to inline it.

10:40.280 --> 10:42.880
Because there's not really being reused.

10:42.880 --> 10:45.360
On the other hand, if you have this being called a bunch of places,

10:45.360 --> 10:47.440
you might not really want to define that everywhere.

10:47.440 --> 10:49.160
You might want to change it in one place.

